2.bug_scenario:
When running ESLint with both the “prefer-const” and “no-undef-init” rules enabled (even if “no-undef-init” is only set to “warn”), `--fix` will transform

    let foo = undefined;

to

    const foo;

instead of leaving the initializer in place.  In other words, the “no-undef-init” fixer removes the `= undefined` after “prefer-const” has made it a `const`, causing invalid code.  Also, ESLint’s built-in fix runner applies fixes in a sort order based on rule ID rather than the order in which rules are listed in your config, so swapping the order in .eslintrc doesn’t help.
